  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nNear-record highs are expected once again Tuesday, all ahead of an \napproaching cold front that is expected to stall in the region. The \nstalled front becomes the focus for active weather through midweek, \nuntil a stronger cold front brings much cooler and drier conditions \nby the end of the week. \n\nThe two primary features to watch are the ridge over the northern \nGulf and the western trough. The Gulf ridge maintains its influence \nover the Midwest Tuesday, as we await the arrival of a cold front to \nthe north. Latest trends continue to slow the cold front with LREF \nsurface temperature spreads of 3-4 degrees through Tuesday \nafternoon. GFS MOS guidance is quite bullish and likely overdone \nwith temperatures well into the 90s and even touching 100 at KSTL, \nespecially with higher dewpoints (mid/upper 60s) drawn in from the \nsouthwest. However, that's not to say it won't be near records, as \nthe lower resolution of the ECMWF ensembles paint low-90s across \nmuch of the region. Highs will likely reach near records once again.\n\nThis pattern begins to break through midweek as surface high \npressure slides east across the northern CONUS Wednesday through \nFriday. The front becomes sandwiched between the northern surface \nhigh and ridging over the southeast CONUS. Meanwhile, the evolution \nof the upper pattern is far less certain as long range global \nguidance have two very different solutions. Both show a Canadian \ntrough tracking southeastward, which has polar origin. Where the two \ndiverge is how they handle the cutoff trough to the west. ECMWF \nmaintains the cutoff low before retrograding it westward later in \nthe week, while the GFS show the low consolidating with the Canadian \ntrough and phasing with the northern stream as is slingshots to the \nnortheast. LREF temperature spreads of around 10 degrees result from \nthis uncertainty in the pattern's evolution. What looks somewhat \npromising is the chance for measurable rainfall considering the\nrecently dry and warm stretch. Though global ensembles show a \nlarge spread in amounts with episodic waves of scattered showers\nand thunderstorms. PWATs are 3-4 standard deviations above \nnormal, running around 2 inches. Fortunately, the front is more \nso quasi- stationary and wavers some as this all transpires. I'd\nexpect efficient rainfall production, though specific locations\nwith higher totals will adjust day-to-day until the front \nclears the area.\n\nBy late week, a strengthening surface high aids to push the front \nthrough with much cooler air filling in behind it. We may even see \ntemperatures dip slightly below normal next weekend with a\ndecrease in LREF IQR spreads (around 5-7 degrees), indicating \nat least decent confidence in cooler, drier trends heading into \nFriday through next weekend. Highs then looks to be 20-30 \ndegrees cooler (50s/60s) by Saturday with mid-30s to low-40s \nSaturday/Sunday mornings. This could be the first potential for \nfrost, especially for cooler, outlying locations.",
